    "budge": {
      "word": "BUDGE",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "To move off; to stir; to walk away. I'll not budge an inch, boy. Shak. The mouse ne'er shunned the cat as they did budge From rascals worse than they. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "Brisk; stirring; jocund. [Obs.] South.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "A kind of fur prepared from lambskin dressed with the wool on; habits.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Lined with budge; hence, scholastic. \"Budge gowns.\" Milton.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        },
        {
          "text": "Austere or stiff, like scholastics. Those budge doctors of the stoic fur. Milton. Budge bachelor, one of a company of men clothed in long gowns lined with budge, who formerly accompanied the lord mayor of London in his inaugural procession. head, the other end being closed by a piece of leather, which is drawn together with strings like a purse. It is used for carrying powder from the magazine to the battery, in siege or seacoast service.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adjective"
        }
      ],
